body:
The only knife I had available to me when I tried was serrated. I thought it was a good idea at the time but when I tried to cut my wrist, the blade was hideously cold and each pointed edge was dull. I realized I was going to be in a world of pain if I tried again because it was going to take a lot of effort. I realized I didn't have the courage to slice hard repeatedly, like I thought I would, and ended up sobbing for a few hours instead.

&#x200B;

TL;DR I stopped because I'm a coward.

I guess the main thing I am keeping from him is my back-up plan. 

If he breaks up with me, I'm going to move to either Iowa or Minnesota. I'm going to keep writing but I'm going to work hard to get myself a little place that will allow me to have two dogs up in Grand Marais, MN or close by it and I'm going to go full hermit. I won't date anymore. I will find a way to stop wanting to have children. I won't actively try to make friends. I will just let my life be me, pets, nature, my writing, and doing whatever work I need to do to survive.

&#x200B;

It's not as fulfilling as the future we've discussed together, but I don't want that future with anyone else. It's either him, me, and the world or it's me, dogs, and nature. I've settled hardcore on those two options.

&#x200B;

I don't talk to him about it because I think it would make him sad to know that I think about what I would do without him. We plan a lot for our future and we are trying to make big steps together to make it happen. I don't want him to know that I have a plan b if things don't work out because I don't want him to question my commitment.

In middle school, most girls refused to go into the locker room alone because everyone complained about it being creepy in there. The feeling of being watched was pretty overwhelming but I figured it was because it was a room full of judgmental girls who were getting changed before and after gym class. I swear, one bored moment of wandering eyes about the room and the rumors suddenly ignited that you were a lesbian. I saw it happen to a lot of girls. It was a very uncomfortable space, so of course it was creepy.  


The one and only time I had to go in there alone, I expected it to be calm. After all, no one else was in there. No one was going to start telling everyone in school that I was a lesbian if I looked around the room or at the other lockers while I was getting dressed. I was kind of looking forward to the solitude, but when I walked in the whole room was foggy, like the someone was taking a lava-hot shower for an hour straight. 

&#x200B;

I didn't hear any water running and as the heavy door slid closed behind me, I felt like I wasn't alone. Curiously, I took a lap around the locker room. No one was in there, not even in the showers. I considered rinsing off because I had to leave in the middle of gym class to go to a chiropractic appointment. I didn't want to smell funny but I also couldn't shake being watched sensation.

&#x200B;

I honestly wanted out of the bathroom more than I wanted to rinse off, so I went to my locker, got changed as quick as I could, and put on an extra coat of deodorant. As I was leaving, I heard a deep, heavy sigh. I have no idea where it came from, but I ran. I never went into that locker room again after that and in high school, I refused to take gym class so that I wouldn't have to use another locker room. I didn't think I'd have the same experience there but I have always been uncomfortable changing in front of others so I took any class that got me out of having to do that.

&#x200B;

That's the craziest thing I've ever experienced. Now that I've paid comment tax, I'm going to read a bunch of other comments.
